## 12. GitHub Projects implementation

All of this is native to GitHub Projects. No new tooling, no new license.

**Fields:**

| Field | Type | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Work Item Type` | Single select | Section 4. The backbone of all reporting. |
| `Class of Service` | Single select | Expedite / Fixed Date / Standard / Intangible |
| `Status` | Single select | The DoW states from Section 3 |
| `Workstream` | Single select | Platform / AI / Automation / Requests / Escalations |
| `Iteration` | Iteration (2 weeks) | Reporting boundary and join key to project teams' sprints. Stamped on **completion**, not on promise. |
| `Committed` | Date | Set on entry to Ready — cycle time start |
| `Completed` | Date | Set on entry to Done — cycle time stop |
| `Blocked Reason` | Single select | Mandatory when Status = Blocked |
| `Target Date` | Date | Fixed Date class only |

The `Committed` and `Completed` stamps can be automated with a GitHub Actions workflow on `projects_v2_item` events, so the data collection is not a manual discipline that decays.

**Views:**

- **Board grouped by Status** — daily working view, WIP limits set per column
- **Board grouped by Work Item Type** — the allocation view; this is the screenshot for the deck
- **Table, `Status != Done`, sorted by `Committed` ascending** — the aging WIP view; the top row is the oldest in-flight item and is discussed first at standup
- **Roadmap view, filtered to Fixed Date** — external commitments and delivery dates
- **Insights** — throughput by iteration grouped by Work Item Type

