## 13. Rollout

Following **STATIK** (Systems Thinking Approach to Introducing Kanban) — the standard method for introducing Kanban to an existing team, which is deliberately evolutionary rather than a process replacement.

| Step | Action | Duration |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Identify sources of dissatisfaction — internal and from our customers | 1 session |
| 2 | **Analyze demand** — backfill 90 days of closed issues with WIT and CoS via the GitHub API; measure actual arrival rates and allocation | ~1 day of scripting |
| 3 | Analyze capability — derive current throughput and cycle-time distributions from the backfill | Same |
| 4 | Model the workflow — validate the DoW in Section 3 against how work actually moves | 1 session |
| 5 | Discover classes of service — validate Section 5 against real examples | Same session |
| 6 | Design the board — build in GitHub Projects | 1 day |
| 7 | Socialize and negotiate — this document, plus the backfilled charts | This conversation |

**Then run it as a shadow board for two iterations** alongside the existing process. Nothing is at risk during that window, and at the end of it we will have two real allocation charts and a first cut at our SLEs. If the artifacts are not better than what Scrum was giving us, we stop and we have lost nothing.

**The sequencing point:** step 2 comes before the decision. We can generate the first allocation chart from historical data *before* changing anything, which means the choice can be made against a real artifact rather than a description of one.

