## 14. Addressing the objections

**"If you look at it from the totally opposite view — no Scrum, no sprints — it begins to break down."**

Correct, and the argument is sound as far as it goes. It establishes that *some* cadence, *some* forecasting mechanism, and *some* commitment discipline are necessary. It does not establish that those must take the form of a sprint commitment. Section 9 supplies the cadences, Section 11 supplies the forecasting, Section 8 supplies the commitment discipline. What we are removing is specifically the fixed two-week batch commitment — the one element that our demand profile makes unsound — and nothing else.

**"All our projects work on the sprint cadence, so we'd just have to speak in sprints on project X while we discuss deadlines and delivery dates on our team."**

Yes — and that is the design, not a compromise in it. The Service Delivery Review runs on a two-week cadence for exactly this reason. Fixed Date class of service is the explicit mechanism for external delivery commitments. Interoperating with iteration-based teams from a flow-based team is a solved problem.

**"Sprints are basically the equivalent of a single point toward a sprint."**

This is the observation that the sprint is functioning as a *reporting unit* rather than a *planning unit*. If that is what it is doing, we should keep it in that role — which is what the Iteration field does — and stop asking it to also serve as a commitment container it cannot support.

**"Will eschewing all current methods in favor of a much more different approach not work?"**

It would not, and we are not proposing it. Kanban explicitly starts with the current process and evolves it; that is a stated principle of the method, not a concession. Roles do not change. The two-week rhythm does not change. Our external reporting boundary does not change. What changes is the contents of the commitment and the artifacts we produce from it.

